{
  "requirement": "A bundled skill MAY put material needed only in specialized situations in files it links to. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** The MAY (a skill may put specialized material in linked files) imposes nothing to check. The MUST (\"each such link says when to read the file\") applies separately in each bundled skill. That makes it an obligation each site upholds on its own, so site reviews cover it. No obligation falls outside both site review and code, so there is no gap under item 8.\n\n**Site query `skills`.** I ran `requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references/skills`. It selects exactly five files:\n- `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md`\n- `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`\n- `assets/skills/habeas-evidence/SKILL.md`\n- `assets/skills/habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md`\n- `assets/skills/habeas-refactor/SKILL.md`\n\nA repo-wide search for `^name: ` front matter finds the same five files and no other skill. The `references/*.md` files have no front matter, so the query correctly leaves them out. It misses no skill and selects nothing out of scope, since the `paths` entry is `assets/skills` and the rule is `minus_metadata`.\n\n**Query snippets.** The match snippet is a document with front matter followed by a heading. The no_match snippet is the same heading with no front matter. Together they show that the query selects on front matter and not on the heading or body. That is adequate for a single-condition query. The requirement has no violation queries.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_say_when_to_read_each_reference_file]` supports the requirement. For each skill in `SKILLS` it walks every file under `references/` that `skill_files` lists. It asserts that each file has an entry in the `links` table. It asserts that the whitespace-collapsed `SKILL.md` contains the \"when to read\" sentence for that file. It covers seven reference files across `habeas`, `habeas-requirements` and `habeas-evidence`. The sentences name the trigger condition, for example \"when adding these steps to an existing workflow\" and \"If authoring fails on configuration…\".\n\n**Not a gap.** The test does not check cross-skill links. The `../habeas/references/ci.md` link in the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-evidence` skills is one example. Each of those links appears in a sentence that says when to use the file, such as recommending CI steps. The test also does not check that `skill_files` lists every file on disk. The obligation is per-site and the site reviews cover it, so these are not adequacy gaps.",
  "references": "5b5a1f7cf171bc78f06adc8576f6e3e3dcb20c5f062af5c778e2b34a76dbfa84",
  "inputs": [
    {
